The Security Council,

Recalling the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations and its previous resolutions, statements of its President, and press statements on the situation in Sudan,

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, unity, territorial integrity, and political independence of Sudan, and to the principles of good-neighbourliness and non-interference in its internal affairs,

Expressing grave alarm at the continuing escalation of hostilities between the Sudanese Armed Forces and the Rapid Support Forces and other armed groups, the spread of violence across Darfur, Kordofan, Khartoum, Gezira, and other states, and the catastrophic humanitarian situation in Sudan,

Condemning in the strongest terms all attacks on civilians and civilian objects, including indiscriminate aerial bombardments, shelling, siege tactics, attacks on hospitals, schools, markets, water and electricity infrastructure, and on humanitarian personnel, convoys, and facilities, and the alarming surge in conflict-related sexual and gender-based violence,

Expressing deep concern at the dire humanitarian situation, including record levels of displacement, food insecurity, famine risk, outbreaks of cholera and other diseases, flooding, and the obstruction of humanitarian access by all parties, and recalling the obligations of all parties under international humanitarian law, including the principles of distinction, proportionality, and precautions in attack, and the prohibition of starvation of civilians as a method of warfare,

Condemning the continued military escalation in and around Al-Fashir, North Darfur, including the siege imposed by the Rapid Support Forces, and stressing the need to protect civilians and allow safe, unhindered humanitarian access to all affected areas,

Deeply concerned by the establishment of a parallel government in Nyala, South Darfur, and by any actions that threaten the sovereignty, unity, territorial integrity, and political independence of Sudan,

Expressing grave concern at reports of external support, arms transfers, and financing that fuel the conflict and undermine the prospects for peace, and calling upon all Member States to refrain from any action that exacerbates tensions or violates relevant arms embargoes,

Welcoming the efforts of the African Union, the Intergovernmental Authority on Development, the United Nations, and other regional and international partners to facilitate dialogue, de-escalation, and a peaceful resolution of the conflict, and recalling the Jeddah Declaration of Commitment to Protect the Civilians of Sudan,

Stressing the importance of an inclusive, civilian-led political process and transition that reflects the aspirations of the Sudanese people, with the full, equal, meaningful, and safe participation of women, youth, and civil society,

Underscoring the need to end impunity for violations of international humanitarian law and human rights law, and emphasizing that those responsible must be held accountable,

Noting the Secretary-General's reports on Sudan and South Sudan and the urgent need for a coordinated international response,

1. Demands that all parties to the conflict immediately cease hostilities and agree to a durable ceasefire and a peaceful resolution of the conflict, and calls upon the Sudanese Armed Forces, the Rapid Support Forces, and all armed groups to de-escalate, exercise restraint, and return to dialogue;

2. Condemns in the strongest terms all attacks on civilians and civilian infrastructure, demands that all parties fully comply with international humanitarian law and international human rights law, and demands that the Rapid Support Forces immediately lift the siege of Al-Fashir and cease all attacks on civilians and humanitarian convoys;

3. Reaffirms the sovereignty, unity, territorial integrity, and political independence of Sudan, rejects the establishment of any parallel government or authority that undermines the unity and sovereignty of Sudan, and calls upon all States not to recognize or support such structures;

4. Demands that all parties ensure safe, rapid, unhindered, and sustained humanitarian access to all people in need throughout Sudan, including through cross-border and cross-line routes, and in this regard calls for the continued and expanded opening of the Adre border crossing and other humanitarian corridors, and demands that all parties remove arbitrary bureaucratic impediments, ensure the safety and security of humanitarian personnel, and facilitate the delivery of food, water, medicine, fuel, and other essential supplies;

5. Reaffirms the obligations of all parties under international humanitarian law, including the prohibition of starvation of civilians as a method of warfare, and demands that all parties protect civilians, including refugees, internally displaced persons, and members of vulnerable groups, and ensure the voluntary, safe, and dignified return of displaced persons when conditions permit;

6. Calls upon all Member States to refrain from providing arms, military support, or financing that would fuel the conflict and exacerbate the suffering of the Sudanese people, to comply with relevant arms embargoes, and to support efforts to combat the illicit transfer and diversion of weapons;

7. Demands the immediate and unconditional release of all persons arbitrarily detained, and calls upon all parties to respect the human rights and fundamental freedoms of all persons, including freedom of expression, peaceful assembly, and the press;

8. Stresses the need to end impunity for serious violations of international humanitarian law and human rights law, including acts that may constitute war crimes and crimes against humanity, and calls upon all parties to cooperate with credible and independent investigations and accountability efforts;

9. Encourages the resumption of an inclusive, civilian-led political process and transition in Sudan, with the full, equal, meaningful, and safe participation of women, youth, and civil society, and welcomes the efforts of the African Union, IGAD, and the United Nations to facilitate such a process;

10. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Security Council within 60 days on the implementation of this resolution, including on the humanitarian situation, access, and the protection of civilians, and decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

