2. Condemns in the strongest terms all attacks on civilians and civilian infrastructure, demands that all parties fully comply with international humanitarian law and international human rights law, and demands that the Rapid Support Forces immediately lift the siege of Al-Fashir and all other sieges and cease all attacks on civilians and humanitarian convoys, and attacks on medical personnel and facilities, and calls on all parties to respect medical neutrality; and demands that all parties, including the Sudanese Armed Forces, immediately cease indiscriminate aerial bombardments and shelling of populated areas;

3. Reaffirms the sovereignty, unity, territorial integrity, and political independence of Sudan, rejects the establishment of any parallel government or authority that undermines the unity and sovereignty of Sudan, demands the immediate dissolution of such parallel structures, and calls upon all States not to recognize, support, or provide any form of assistance to such structures;

4. Demands that all parties ensure safe, rapid, unhindered, and sustained humanitarian access to all people in need throughout Sudan, especially to Al-Fashir and other besieged areas, including through cross-border and cross-line routes, including through the Adre crossing with Chad and the Renk crossing with South Sudan and other humanitarian corridors, in full respect for Sudan's sovereignty and territorial integrity and in coordination with the Government of Sudan and relevant humanitarian actors, and in this regard calls for the continued and expanded opening of the Adre border crossing and other humanitarian corridors, and demands that all parties remove arbitrary bureaucratic impediments, ensure the safety and security of humanitarian personnel, allow the United Nations and humanitarian partners to deliver assistance without arbitrary delays, taxation, or illegal fees, and facilitate the delivery of food, water, medicine, fuel, and other essential supplies;

5. Reaffirms the obligations of all parties under international humanitarian law, including the prohibition of starvation of civilians as a method of warfare, and demands that all parties protect civilians, including refugees, internally displaced persons, and members of vulnerable groups, and ensure the voluntary, safe, and dignified return of displaced persons when conditions permit; and demands that all parties immediately cease all forms of conflict-related sexual and gender-based violence, ensure safe access to medical and psychosocial services for survivors, hold perpetrators accountable, and ensure the full, equal, meaningful, and safe participation of women and youth in all peace and political processes;

6. Demands that all Member States comply fully with the arms embargo on Darfur imposed by resolution 1591 (2005), as renewed, and with all other relevant arms embargoes, and calls upon all Member States to refrain from providing arms, military support, or financing to the parties to the conflict, in particular the Rapid Support Forces and other armed groups, that would fuel the conflict and exacerbate the suffering of the Sudanese people, including the transfer of arms, the provision of foreign fighters or mercenaries, and any other external military support; further calls upon all Member States to respect the sovereignty, unity, territorial integrity, and political independence of Sudan, to refrain from any action that exacerbates tensions, to support efforts to combat the illicit transfer and diversion of weapons, including through proxies and intermediaries, and to exercise due diligence and transparent end-use controls, in accordance with international law and relevant Security Council resolutions, and without prejudice to the sovereign right of the Government of Sudan to protect its people and secure its borders in accordance with international law;

7. Demands the immediate and unconditional release of all persons arbitrarily detained, and calls upon all parties to respect the human rights and fundamental freedoms of all persons, including freedom of expression, peaceful assembly, and the press;

8. Stresses the need to end impunity for serious violations of international humanitarian law and human rights law, including acts that may constitute war crimes and crimes against humanity, and calls upon all parties to cooperate with credible and independent investigations and accountability efforts, including through relevant Security Council-mandated mechanisms and other impartial mechanisms consistent with international law, while respecting Sudan's judicial sovereignty;

9. Encourages the resumption of an inclusive, civilian-led, Sudanese-owned and Sudanese-led political process and transition in Sudan, with the full, equal, meaningful, and safe participation of women, youth, and civil society, and welcomes the efforts of the African Union, IGAD, the League of Arab States, and the United Nations to facilitate such a process, with the African Union and IGAD playing a leading role in close coordination with the United Nations and other partners, and calls upon all external actors to refrain from interference that undermines Sudan's sovereignty and the political process, and calls upon the Secretary-General to continue to use his good offices, in coordination with the African Union, IGAD, the League of Arab States, and other regional partners, to support such a process and a durable ceasefire;

10. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Security Council within 60 days on the implementation of this resolution, including on the humanitarian situation, access, the protection of civilians, obstacles to humanitarian access, attacks on humanitarian and medical personnel and facilities, and reports of external support, arms transfers, and financing that fuel the conflict, including violations of the arms embargo, drawing as appropriate on information from the 1591 Sanctions Committee and its Panel of Experts, and decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

